WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.484
Welcome back to HarbourLink Logistics.

2
00:00:02.484 --> 00:00:05.418
This annual refresher takes only a few minutes.

3
00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:10.126
Its purpose is simple: notice hazards early,
choose a safe action, and

4
00:00:10.126 --> 00:00:11.695
report what needs fixing.

5
00:00:11.695 --> 00:00:13.055
You know the workplace.

6
00:00:13.055 --> 00:00:15.362
Today, look at it with fresh eyes.

7
00:00:15.362 --> 00:00:18.555
Small changes in a busy shift can create a new
risk.

8
00:00:18.555 --> 00:00:23.363
If anything in this example differs from your
site procedure, stop and

9
00:00:23.363 --> 00:00:25.366
follow your approved workplace instruction.

10
00:00:26.866 --> 00:00:32.463
Before you lift, drive, stack, clean, or enter a
work area, pause.

11
00:00:32.463 --> 00:00:36.748
Look at the route, the load, the equipment, and
the people nearby.

12
00:00:36.748 --> 00:00:39.611
Ask: what has changed since my last shift?

13
00:00:39.611 --> 00:00:41.428
A clear route can become blocked.

14
00:00:41.428 --> 00:00:43.537
A dry floor can become slippery.

15
00:00:43.537 --> 00:00:45.123
A stable stack can move.

16
00:00:45.123 --> 00:00:48.289
This quick pause helps you act before someone
gets hurt.

17
00:00:48.289 --> 00:00:51.547
Pause and think: What changed in your work area
today?

18
00:00:53.047 --> 00:00:57.877
Walkways, forklift routes, and emergency exits
must stay clear.

19
00:00:57.877 --> 00:01:03.117
Never leave cartons, pallets, cables, or
equipment where people travel.

20
00:01:03.117 --> 00:01:05.644
Keep fire doors and exit signs visible.

21
00:01:05.644 --> 00:01:10.117
If you find an obstruction, protect the area and
arrange removal using

22
00:01:10.117 --> 00:01:11.235
your site process.

23
00:01:11.235 --> 00:01:14.745
Do not step around it and assume the next person
will notice.

24
00:01:14.745 --> 00:01:18.747
Pause and think: Which route must stay clear
during your next task?

25
00:01:20.247 --> 00:01:22.216
A spill needs immediate control.

26
00:01:22.216 --> 00:01:26.732
Warn others, isolate the area, and use the
correct spill procedure.

27
00:01:26.732 --> 00:01:28.710
Do not walk through an unknown liquid.

28
00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.282
Check stacked goods before working nearby.

29
00:01:31.282 --> 00:01:35.289
Leaning, damaged, or overloaded stacks need
trained help.

30
00:01:35.289 --> 00:01:37.987
Never pull one item from an unstable stack.

31
00:01:37.987 --> 00:01:40.971
Stop, secure the area, and report it.

32
00:01:42.471 --> 00:01:45.697
Forklifts and pedestrians need clear separation.

33
00:01:45.697 --> 00:01:48.184
Use marked walkways and crossing points.

34
00:01:48.184 --> 00:01:52.180
Make eye contact with the operator where your
procedure requires it.

35
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:55.238
Never enter a blind spot or walk under a raised
load.

36
00:01:55.238 --> 00:01:59.986
Operators must follow speed limits, sound
warnings where required, and stop when

37
00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:01.964
the route is not clear.

38
00:02:01.964 --> 00:02:05.548
Pause and think: Where is the nearest blind
corner on your route?

39
00:02:07.048 --> 00:02:10.867
Wear the PPE required for the task and check it
before use.

40
00:02:10.867 --> 00:02:12.639
Replace damaged PPE.

41
00:02:12.639 --> 00:02:17.006
Use tools, ladders, guards, and controls only as
trained.

42
00:02:17.006 --> 00:02:19.378
Do not bypass a guard to save time.

43
00:02:19.378 --> 00:02:24.693
If equipment behaves differently, stop it
safely, label or isolate it as

44
00:02:24.693 --> 00:02:27.350
required, and tell the responsible person.

45
00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:34.034
Report hazards, near misses, injuries, and
damaged equipment promptly.

46
00:02:34.034 --> 00:02:38.991
Give clear facts: the location, the hazard, the
immediate control, and who

47
00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:40.643
needs to follow up.

48
00:02:40.643 --> 00:02:43.712
Do not include unnecessary personal information.

49
00:02:43.712 --> 00:02:47.355
A report is useful only when someone owns the
action and checks

50
00:02:47.355 --> 00:02:48.873
that the risk is controlled.

51
00:02:48.873 --> 00:02:52.434
Pause and think: Who receives a hazard report on
your shift?

52
00:02:53.934 --> 00:02:59.910
Before you begin, use the five-second check:
route clear, load stable, equipment

53
00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.898
safe, PPE ready, and people protected.

54
00:03:02.898 --> 00:03:06.409
If one answer is no, stop and fix it or ask for

55
00:03:06.409 --> 00:03:06.702
help.

56
00:03:06.702 --> 00:03:09.734
You are not expected to take an unsafe shortcut.

57
00:03:09.734 --> 00:03:13.727
Thank you for helping every HarbourLink
colleague finish the shift safely.

58
00:03:13.727 --> 00:03:17.740
Continue to the hazard activity and the five-
question practice quiz.

59
00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:20.175
The practice pass mark is eighty percent.
